    "area": "1.9M km²",
    "sum": "Stretching 5,000 km along the equator across over 17,000 volcanic islands, Indonesia is the world's largest archipelagic state and fourth most populous nation (~282 million people). Bridging Asia and Australia, the archipelago commands all three strategic chokepoints linking the Indian and Pacific Oceans: the Malacca, Sunda, and Lombok Straits. Heir to the ancient maritime empires of Srivijaya and Majapahit, the diverse island chain was welded together under Dutch colonial rule before proclaiming independence in 1945.\n\nGuided by 'Unity in Diversity' (Bhinneka Tunggal Ika), Indonesia is the world's largest Muslim-majority democracy and practices an independent 'rowing between two reefs' foreign policy. As Southeast Asia's largest economy and sole G20 member, Jakarta has leveraged the planet's largest nickel reserves to compel global automakers and battery firms to build local smelters. In 2025–2026 under President Prabowo Subianto, Indonesia joined BRICS while advancing the gradual relocation of its capital from sinking, congested Jakarta to Nusantara in East Kalimantan.",
