    "sum": "Malaysia is split into two halves separated by over 600 km of the South China Sea: Peninsular Malaysia on the Asian mainland and East Malaysia (Sabah and Sarawak) on Borneo. While Peninsular Malaysia flanks the northern shore of the Strait of Malacca along the Titiwangsa mountain spine, East Malaysia holds vast rainforests and offshore oil and gas fields. Heir to the fifteenth-century Malacca Sultanate and later British colonial rule, the Federation of Malaysia formed in 1963 by uniting Malaya with Sabah and Sarawak.\n\nGoverned as a federal parliamentary democracy with a unique rotating monarchy among nine state sultans, Malaysia balances a multi-ethnic society of Malay Muslims, Chinese, Indians, and Bornean peoples. Backed by hydrocarbon revenues from state oil giant Petronas, it built a world-class semiconductor hub in Penang and a booming AI data-center cluster in Johor next to Singapore. In 2025–2026 (serving as 2025 ASEAN Chair), Malaysia is advancing the Johor–Singapore Special Economic Zone and pragmatic non-alignment as it nears high-income status.",
