    "area": "5,770 km²",
    "sum": "Nestled on the northern coast of Borneo, the Sultanate of Brunei Darussalam spans 5,765 km², with pristine equatorial rainforests still blanketing over 70% of its land. In a striking geographic quirk, Brunei is split into two enclaves—its main western territory and eastern Temburong—by Malaysia's Sarawak state while fronting onto the oil-rich southern South China Sea. Once a fifteenth-century maritime empire holding sway across coastal Borneo, Brunei shrank under European encroachment and British protectorate rule until full independence in 1984.\n\nToday, Brunei is Southeast Asia's sole absolute monarchy, ruled by Sultan Hassanal Bolkiah under the state philosophy of 'Malay Islamic Monarchy' (MIB). Abundant offshore crude oil and liquefied natural gas (LNG) give its 450,000 residents ASEAN's second-highest per-capita income, zero personal income tax, and cradle-to-grave welfare. In 2025–2026, the sultanate's priority under Wawasan 2035 is diversifying into downstream petrochemicals—led by Chinese investment at Pulau Muara Besar—as mature oilfields decline.",
