    "sum": "Shaped like a teardrop off the southern tip of India, the island of Sri Lanka (65,610 km²) features a central mountain massif surrounded by coastal plains. Separated from India by the shallow 30 km Palk Strait while sitting right astride the main East–West shipping route across the Indian Ocean, it combines continental proximity with maritime centrality. Two millennia of history shaped a Sinhalese Buddhist majority alongside a northern and eastern Hindu Tamil minority, followed by British colonial rule (as Ceylon) until independence in 1948 and a 26-year civil war (1983–2009).\n\nAfter defeating the Tamil Tigers in 2009, heavy external borrowing, pandemic shocks, and policy blunders triggered Sri Lanka's 2022 sovereign debt default and a popular uprising that ousted the Rajapaksa family. In September 2024, voters elected leftist leader Anura Kumara Dissanayake as president on an anti-corruption platform while maintaining the IMF-backed debt-restructuring program. In 2025–2026, Colombo carefully balances competing geopolitical courtship from India (investing in Colombo Port and energy) and China (operator of Hambantota Port under a 99-year lease).",
