    "sum": "Perched on the southern slopes of the Eastern Himalayas, the Kingdom of Bhutan (38,394 km²) rises steeply from 200-meter subtropical foothills in the south to glaciated peaks above 7,500 meters in the north. Landlocked between Chinese Tibet and India, its isolated mountain valleys enabled the kingdom to resist foreign conquest throughout history. Unified under the Wangchuck monarchy in 1907, Bhutan peacefully transitioned from absolute royal rule to a constitutional monarchy in 2008.\n\nSteeped in Vajrayana Buddhist tradition, Bhutan pioneered 'Gross National Happiness' (GNH) and preserves over 70% forest cover as one of the world's few carbon-negative countries. Geopolitically, Thimphu binds its security and economy closely to India—maintaining no formal diplomatic relations with China—while holding border talks with Beijing after the 2017 Doklam standoff. In 2025–2026, King Jigme Khesar Namgyel Wangchuck is launching the 'Gelephu Mindfulness City' special economic zone on the Indian border to attract green investment and stem youth emigration.",
