    "pop": "~20.6M",
    "area": "2.72M km²",
    "sum": "Spanning 2.72 million km² from the Caspian Sea to the Altai and Tian Shan mountains, Kazakhstan is the world's ninth-largest country and largest landlocked nation on Earth. Vast grasslands (the Kazakh Steppe) and semi-arid basins dominate its continental landscape, supporting a sparse population of ~20.6 million people (~7.5 people/km²). Long the domain of nomadic steppe horsemen before Russian imperial and Soviet rule—hosting the Baikonur Cosmodrome and Semipalatinsk nuclear site—Kazakhstan was the last Soviet republic to declare independence in December 1991.\n\nTo anchor state control over its northern provinces bordering Russia, the government relocated the capital in 1997 from southeastern Almaty to northern Astana. Endowed with giant oilfields and the world's largest uranium output (~40% of global supply), President Kassym-Jomart Tokayev practices 'multi-vector diplomacy' balancing Russia, China, and the West. In 2025–2026, Kazakhstan is expanding the Trans-Caspian 'Middle Corridor' linking China to Europe across the Caspian Sea while confronting water scarcity and reliance on Russian oil transit pipelines.",
