    "pop": "~11M",
    "area": "83,600 km²",
    "sum": "Positioned on the southeastern rim of the Arabian Peninsula right at the strategic bend of the Strait of Hormuz, the United Arab Emirates (UAE) features flat coastal salt plains and rolling sand dunes backed by the rugged Hajar Mountains in the east. Commanding both a long Persian Gulf shoreline and a vital Indian Ocean frontage at Fujairah on the Gulf of Oman, the country serves as a natural maritime pivot linking Asia, the Middle East, and Europe. Once a collection of impoverished pearl-diving sheikhdoms under British maritime protection known as the Trucial States, seven emirates united in 1971 under founding father Sheikh Zayed to form a sovereign federation.\n\nBuilt on a complementary partnership between oil-rich Abu Dhabi and commercially dynamic Dubai, the UAE has forged a hyper-globalized society where expatriates make up nearly 90% of the population. In 2025–2026, now a member of the expanded BRICS bloc, the federation punches far above its size by pouring tens of billions of dollars into sovereign AI compute infrastructure, acquiring ports across Africa and the Red Sea, and preserving the 2020 Abraham Accords with Israel. Its core vulnerabilities remain extreme reliance on seawater desalination and geopolitical exposure to Iran across the Persian Gulf, laid bare when Iranian missiles and drones struck the UAE during the 2026 war.",
