    "sum": "Jutting northward into the Persian Gulf like a thumb from the Arabian Peninsula, Qatar is a flat, arid limestone peninsula of 11,600 km² with a single 87 km land border shared with Saudi Arabia to the south. Devoid of mountains, forests, or arable soil, the peninsula supported only a small population of pearl divers and Bedouin herders before gaining independence from British protection in 1971. Its destiny transformed in the 1990s when Doha pioneered large-scale Liquefied Natural Gas (LNG) exports from the offshore North Field—the world's largest non-associated gas reservoir—catapulting the emirate to the top of global per-capita wealth rankings.\n\nRuled by the Al Thani family under Emir Tamim bin Hamad, Qatar punches far above its geographic size by combining vast sovereign wealth, the global reach of the Al Jazeera media network, and hosting the largest US air base in the Middle East at Al Udeid. Having weathered the 2017–2021 blockade by four Arab neighbors and hosted the 2022 FIFA World Cup, Doha serves in 2025–2026 as an indispensable diplomatic mediator in Gaza ceasefire talks, US–Iran backchannels, and conflicts from Afghanistan to Africa. Its existential vulnerabilities remain total dependence on the Strait of Hormuz for LNG shipping and coastal desalination for freshwater; when Hormuz was blocked and Iranian strikes hit Ras Laffan in 2026, QatarEnergy declared force majeure on LNG contracts.",
