    "pop": "~1.6M",
    "area": "~785 km²",
    "sum": "The Persian Gulf's only island nation and the smallest Arab state at roughly 785 km² (steadily expanded through land reclamation), Bahrain comprises an archipelago of over 30 natural and artificial islands nestled in a shallow bay between Saudi Arabia and the Qatar Peninsula. Blessed historically with rare freshwater submarine springs and a central Gulf location, the islands anchored the ancient Bronze Age trading civilization of Dilmun 4,000 years ago and later dominated the global natural pearl trade. After becoming the first Arab Gulf state to strike oil in 1932 and gaining independence from Britain in 1971, Bahrain was physically linked to mainland Saudi Arabia by the 25 km King Fahd Causeway in 1986.\n\nDomestically, Bahrain is defined by a delicate sectarian balance in which the Sunni Al Khalifa royal family governs a citizen population with a Shia majority—a fault line that erupted in mass protests during the 2011 Arab Spring before Gulf troops intervened. Geopolitically in 2025–2026, Manama serves as the indispensable homeport of the US Navy's Fifth Fleet, a signatory to the Abraham Accords with Israel, and a partner in the US-UK-Bahrain Comprehensive Security Integration and Prosperity Agreement (CIPA). With modest onshore oil reserves, Bahrain's core challenge is managing high public debt while relying heavily on Saudi economic and security backing; during the 2026 war, Bahrain, home of the Fifth Fleet, was also hit by Iranian missiles and drones.",
