    "pop": "~34K",
    "area": "61 km²",
    "sum": "Perched atop the sheer limestone crags of Mount Titano (739 meters) on the northeastern slopes of the Apennines, the Republic of San Marino is a 61 km² enclave completely surrounded by Italy just 10 kilometers from the Adriatic coast. Founded in 301 AD by a Christian stonemason named Marinus fleeing Roman persecution, it stands as the world's oldest surviving sovereign republic, governed by a constitution dating to 1600. When Napoleon offered to expand its territory in 1797, its regents wisely declined—recognizing that staying tiny was the secret to survival.\n\nBecause San Marino sheltered Italian revolutionary Giuseppe Garibaldi in 1849, the newly unified Kingdom of Italy guaranteed the mountain republic's independence by treaty in 1862. In 2025–2026, this nation of 34,000 citizens—still led by two Captains Regent elected every six months—enjoys high prosperity rooted in precision manufacturing and tourism while advancing its Association Agreement with the EU. Its chief priorities remain modernizing its banking sector and integrating smoothly into the European Single Market.",
