    "pop": "~570K",
    "area": "316 km²",
    "sum": "Positioned dead center in the Mediterranean narrows—93 kilometers south of Sicily and 288 kilometers north of Tunisia—the limestone archipelago of Malta commands the chokepoint dividing the western and eastern Mediterranean. Blessed with deep, naturally fortified harbors, the islands served as the bastion of the Knights Hospitaller during the Great Siege of 1565 against the Ottomans and later as Britain's premier Mediterranean naval fortress. In World War II, Malta withstood over 3,000 Axis bombing raids before gaining independence in 1964.\n\nAfter the last British base closed in 1979, Malta enshrined military neutrality in its constitution in 1980, joining the EU in 2004 and the Eurozone in 2008. In 2025–2026, the EU's smallest member state posts the bloc's fastest GDP growth (~5% annually), powered by Europe's largest shipping registry, tourism, aviation maintenance, and regulated online gaming (iGaming). Its chief challenges are extreme natural freshwater scarcity, urban congestion on limited land, and frontline maritime migration from North Africa.",
