    "area": "1,400 km²",
    "sum": "Rising as 18 jagged volcanic basalt islands out of the stormy North Atlantic, the Faroe Islands sit squarely inside the maritime triangle halfway between Scotland, Iceland, and Norway. Settled by Norse Vikings in the 9th century—who founded the Løgting parliament in Tórshavn, one of the world's oldest legislative assemblies—the archipelago passed from Norwegian rule to the Danish crown in 1814. Following friendly British occupation during World War II while mainland Denmark was held by Germany, the Faroes gained extensive home-rule autonomy within the Kingdom of Denmark in 1948.\n\nCrucially, the Faroese chose to remain outside the European Union when Denmark joined in 1973 in order to retain sovereign control over their rich 200-nautical-mile fishing zone. In 2025–2026, this community of 54,000 people enjoys a higher GDP per capita (~$71,500) than mainland Denmark and near-zero unemployment (~1%) thanks to world-leading fjord salmon farming, while serving as a vital NATO radar sentinel in the strategic GIUK Gap. Its main challenge is diversifying an economy where seafood accounts for over 90% of exports.",
