    "sum": "Straddling the Mid-Atlantic Ridge where the North American and Eurasian plates pull apart just south of the Arctic Circle, Iceland is a land of fire and ice featuring over 30 active volcanic systems and glaciers covering 11% of its surface. Settled by Norse navigators in 874 AD—who founded the Alþingi in 930, the world's oldest surviving parliament—the island later passed under Norwegian and Danish rule for centuries. After Allied forces garrisoned the strategic island during World War II, Iceland formally proclaimed an independent republic in 1944.\n\nAlthough it is the only NATO ally with no standing military (operating only a Coast Guard), Iceland became a founding NATO member in 1949 because it sits as an 'unsinkable aircraft carrier' dead center in the GIUK Gap. In 2025–2026, this nation of 390,000 people generates a GDP per capita above $84,000 powered by 100% renewable hydro and geothermal energy, high-tech fisheries, aluminum smelting, and tourism. At the same time, authorities are managing recurring volcanic fissure eruptions on the Reykjanes Peninsula near Grindavík.",
