    "pop": "~9.6M",
    "area": "93,000 km²",
    "sum": "Occupying the flat floor of the Pannonian Basin in Central Europe and bisected by the Danube and Tisza Rivers, Hungary is a table-flat plain whose steppe ecology drew nomadic Magyar horsemen to settle here in the late 9th century. Founded as a Christian kingdom in the year 1000, historic Hungary's frontiers expanded for nearly a millennium up to the protective crest of the Carpathian Mountain arc and the Adriatic coast. However, the 1920 Treaty of Trianon after World War I stripped Hungary of 71% of its territory, two-thirds of its population, its entire mountain defense ring, and its seacoast.\n\nThat geographic amputation—leaving millions of ethnic Hungarians in neighboring countries—paired with a unique Uralic language, forged a fiercely sovereign national outlook. In 2025–2026, while a NATO and EU member, Budapest pursues an 'Eastern Opening' strategy: maintaining energy ties with Russia while turning Hungary into Europe's top manufacturing bridge pairing German luxury automakers with massive Chinese EV and battery gigafactories (CATL, BYD). Its main challenges are fiscal deficits and frozen EU funds.",
