    "pop": "~2.1M",
    "area": "20,300 km²",
    "sum": "Covering just 20,270 km² with 2.1 million people, Slovenia sits at the exact crossroads where four European landscapes meet: the Alps in the north, the Pannonian Basin in the east, the Dinaric Karst in the south, and the Adriatic coast in the southwest. Ruled for centuries within the Habsburg Austrian Empire before joining Yugoslavia in 1918, the Slovenes developed a deeply Central European industrial culture. When Yugoslavia fractured in June 1991, Slovenia's distance from Belgrade and rugged terrain allowed it to secure independence in the brief 'Ten-Day War.'\n\nEscaping the devastating Balkan wars of the 1990s, Slovenia emerged as the wealthiest republic of the former Yugoslavia—joining the EU and NATO in 2004 and adopting the Euro in 2007. In 2025–2026, Slovenia boasts a PPP per-capita GDP (~$55,300) rivaling Western Europe and one of the world's lowest income-inequality rates, powered by pharmaceuticals, auto components, the Adriatic port of Koper, and Alpine tourism. Its chief priorities are expanding rail freight capacity from Koper and managing demographic aging.",
