    "pop": "~3.85M",
    "area": "56,600 km²",
    "sum": "Shaped like a sweeping boomerang wrapping around Bosnia and Herzegovina, Croatia unites two geographic worlds: its northern wing stretches across the Central European plains of Slavonia, while its southern wing hugs the Dalmatian coast and over 1,200 Adriatic islands. Separating the inland heartland around Zagreb from the coast is the rugged limestone barrier of the Dinaric Alps. Situated along the historic frontier between Habsburg Austria-Hungary, Venice, and the Ottoman Balkans, Croatia declared independence from Yugoslavia in 1991 and fought a four-year war (1991–1995) to secure its borders.\n\nIn the decades since, Zagreb completed full Western integration: joining NATO in 2009, the EU in 2013, and both the Eurozone and Schengen Area on January 1, 2023. In 2025–2026, Croatia ranks among the EU's fastest-growing economies (~3.5%), propelled by Adriatic tourism (>21 million visitors), regional LNG energy imports via Krk Island, EU recovery funds, and electric hypercar maker Rimac. Its chief long-term challenges remain demographic aging and labor shortages.",
