    "pop": "~620K",
    "area": "13,800 km²",
    "sum": "Perched along the Adriatic coast of the Western Balkans, Montenegro—literally the \"Black Mountain\"—is defined by sheer karst limestone peaks plunging straight into the sea and deep river canyons. Shielded by the forbidding mountain fortress around its royal capital of Cetinje, local Orthodox clans preserved a rare degree of autonomy for centuries against the Ottoman Empire. Following the breakup of Yugoslavia, Montenegro peacefully dissolved its union with Serbia in a 2006 referendum to restore its independence.\n\nContemporary Montenegrin society balances a pro-Western civic identity with the roughly one-third of citizens who identify as ethnic Serbs tied to the Serbian Orthodox Church. Its 2017 accession to NATO closed the final non-Allied stretch of the northern Mediterranean coast, ending Moscow's hopes of securing naval access in the Bay of Kotor. Today, as the frontrunner candidate for European Union membership, Montenegro thrives on superyacht tourism and green power exports while managing the vulnerabilities of a micro-economy.",
