    "pop": "~1.8M",
    "area": "25,700 km²",
    "sum": "Located in the landlocked heart of the Balkan Peninsula, North Macedonia is defined by the fertile Vardar River valley slicing north to south between mountain ranges exceeding 2,500 meters and ancient Lake Ohrid. From antiquity through the Roman, Byzantine, and Ottoman empires, the Vardar corridor has served as the sole natural overland highway cutting through the Balkan mountains to link Central Europe with the Aegean Sea. When Yugoslavia disintegrated in 1991, the republic achieved independence peacefully, avoiding the devastating wars that engulfed its neighbors.\n\nNorth Macedonian society operates a power-sharing model where the Orthodox Slavic Macedonian majority governs alongside an ethnic Albanian Muslim community comprising roughly a quarter of the population under the 2001 Ohrid Agreement. In 2019, the country adopted the name \"Republic of North Macedonia\" under the Prespa Agreement, resolving a decades-long dispute with Greece and joining NATO in 2020. Today, Skopje functions as a vital transport and automotive manufacturing crossroads in Southeastern Europe, though its EU accession talks remain slowed by constitutional demands from Bulgaria.",
