    "pop": "~14M",
    "area": "115,000 km²",
    "sum": "Extending northward from sandy Gulf of Guinea lagoons through the 'Dahomey Gap'—a climatic corridor where the West African rainforest breaks into coastal savanna—up to the Niger River valley, the Republic of Benin is shaped like a natural geographic keyhole into the Sahel. From the 17th to the late 19th century, its central plateau was the seat of the formidable Kingdom of Dahomey—famed for the UNESCO-listed Royal Palaces of Abomey and the elite all-female Agojie ('Dahomey Amazons') warrior regiment—while coastal Ouidah served as both the spiritual cradle of Vodun (Voodoo) and a major transatlantic slave port. After gaining independence from France in 1960 as Dahomey and enduring a Marxist-Leninist military regime under Mathieu Kérékou (who renamed the country Benin in 1975), Benin pioneered Francophone Africa's wave of democratization via a peaceful National Conference in 1990.\n\nUnder businessman-turned-President Patrice Talon (in office from 2016 heading into the 2026 presidential transition), Benin has emerged as one of West Africa's standout industrializing stories. By banning raw cotton and cashew exports to mandate local textile and food processing inside the flagship Glo-Djigbé Industrial Zone (GDIZ) and inaugurating the nearly 2,000 km Niger–Benin crude oil export pipeline at Sèmè-Kpodji in 2024, Benin has sustained 6%+ annual GDP growth. At the same time, Cotonou must navigate diplomatic frictions with Niger's military junta over border closures and defend its northern W–Arly–Pendjari national park frontier against jihadist incursions from Burkina Faso.",
