    "pop": "~525K",
    "area": "4,030 km²",
    "sum": "Situated in the mid-Atlantic Ocean roughly 600 km west of Senegal's Cap-Vert Peninsula, the Republic of Cabo Verde is a horseshoe-shaped volcanic archipelago of 10 islands (nine inhabited), divided into the northern windward Barlavento and southern leeward Sotavento groups. Uninhabited when Portuguese explorers arrived in 1456, the islands became the Atlantic world's first European colonial crossroads for maritime trade and enslaved Africans, giving birth to a distinctive Afro-Portuguese Crioulo culture and the soulful UNESCO-listed Morna musical tradition. Lying at the same latitude as the arid Sahelian belt on the mainland, the islands suffered catastrophic multi-year droughts and famines from the 18th to mid-20th centuries, driving waves of emigration to New England and Europe before independence in 1975.\n\nAt independence in 1975, economists widely dismissed resource-barren Cabo Verde as economically unviable due to its lack of permanent rivers, mineral wealth, or arable land. Instead, by building one of Africa's most transparent multiparty parliamentary democracies, investing heavily in education and healthcare, and solving its water crisis through seawater desalination, Cabo Verde climbed solidly into middle-income status (~$4,800 nominal per capita; >$11,200 PPP). Today the archipelago thrives on year-round European beach resort tourism (welcoming over one million visitors annually to Sal and Boa Vista), maritime services across its 800,000-km² ocean zone, and remittances from a global diaspora larger than its domestic population of 600,000.",
