    "sum": "Straddling the Equator in the heart of the Gulf of Guinea roughly 240 km off the coast of Gabon, the Democratic Republic of São Tomé and Príncipe is Africa's second-smallest sovereign nation (964 km²), formed by two emerald volcanic islands along the submarine Cameroon Volcanic Line. Uninhabited when Portuguese explorers arrived around 1470, the islands became the world's original tropical plantation colony worked by enslaved Africans—first dominating 16th-century sugar production and later becoming the planet's #1 cocoa producer in the early 1900s across sprawling estate towns known as *roças*. Following Portugal's Carnation Revolution, the archipelago gained independence in 1975 and in 1990 spearheaded Africa's democratic wave by adopting a peaceful multiparty parliamentary system.\n\nHome to ~235,000 citizens sharing a cohesive Lusophone Creole culture, São Tomé and Príncipe enjoys durable democratic freedoms and complete insulation from mainland Central African conflicts. Celebrated by biologists as the 'Galápagos of Africa' for its extraordinary concentration of endemic birds and plants—and dramatic volcanic spires like needle-shaped Pico Cão Grande—the entire island of Príncipe is a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve anchoring boutique eco-tourism. While the Euro-pegged economy relies on gourmet single-origin organic cocoa ('Chocolate Island'), tourism, and foreign aid, two decades of deep-water oil exploration in its Joint Development Zone with Nigeria have yet to yield commercial production.",
