        "Behind a dry 50–150 km coastal strip cooled by the Benguela Current, the land climbs up a dramatic escarpment (Serra da Chela) onto the central Bié Plateau, peaking at 2,620 m at Morro de Moco. During the 1975–2002 civil war, this geography split the country between the MPLA-held coastal oil cities and UNITA's highland strongholds around Huambo and the eastern diamond fields."
