    "pop": "~94K",
    "area": "442 km²",
    "sum": "Positioned right at the strategic northeastern bend of the Lesser Antilles arc where the Atlantic Ocean meets the Caribbean Sea, Antigua and Barbuda comprises two main inhabited islands—Antigua (281 km², home to 97% of the population) and flat coral Barbuda (161 km², lying 48 km to the north)—plus the tiny uninhabited volcanic islet of Redonda. Unlike the towering volcanic islands further south, Antigua and Barbuda feature low-lying limestone and coral terrain indented by dozens of deep natural coves and famously billed as having \"365 white-sand beaches—one for every day of the year.\" Because English Harbour on Antigua's southern coast offered an almost completely landlocked hurricane shelter, 18th-century Admiral Horatio Nelson made it the fortified headquarters and shipyard of the British Royal Navy's Leeward Islands fleet (now the UNESCO World Heritage site of Nelson's Dockyard).\n\nIndependent from Britain since 1981 as a Commonwealth parliamentary realm, Antigua and Barbuda boasts one of the highest standards of living in the Eastern Caribbean, with a per-capita GDP surpassing $21,500 (~$31,000 PPP). Under Prime Minister Gaston Browne, the nation of 94,000 serves as the yachting and regatta capital of the Caribbean (hosting Antigua Sailing Week), a regional aviation hub at V.C. Bird International Airport, and a vocal climate-diplomacy leader that hosted the 4th UN Conference on Small Island Developing States (SIDS4) in May 2024. Its foremost existential challenges are chronic natural aridity and extreme vulnerability to Category 5 hurricanes—dramatically illustrated in September 2017 when Hurricane Irma destroyed 95% of Barbuda's buildings and forced the temporary evacuation of the entire island.",
