        "Argentina's entire western frontier is walled off by the highest segment of the Andean cordillera—topped by 6,961 m Mount Aconcagua, the tallest peak in both the Western and Southern Hemispheres—while the windswept steppes of Patagonia guard the south. Situated thousands of kilometers from global geopolitical flashpoints and separated from neighbors by towering mountains or wide rivers, Argentina faces zero overland invasion threat."
