        "Covering over 240,000 km² in the northwest, the semi-arid Gran Chaco plain forms a harsh natural buffer of thorny scrubland and seasonal mudflats that famously enabled Paraguayan soldiers to outmaneuver Bolivian forces during the 1932–1935 Chaco War. East of the Paraguay River, by contrast, gentle rolling hills and deep, iron-rich basaltic soils (terra rossa) provide ideal terrain for mechanized soybean farming and cattle ranching."
