        "Brazil is shielded by two continental-scale natural buffers: over 5 million km² of dense Amazon rainforest across the north and northwest, and 7,491 km of open South Atlantic Ocean along the east. In 1960, President Juscelino Kubitschek executed a bold geostrategic pivot by relocating the national capital from coastal Rio de Janeiro 1,000 km inland to the modernist planned city of Brasília on the central Cerrado plateau, securing the seat of government and catalyzing interior settlement."
