    "pop": "~630K",
    "area": "164,000 km²",
    "sum": "Nestled between Guyana and French Guiana on South America's northeastern Atlantic shoulder, Suriname is the continent's smallest independent country by both area (164,000 km²) and population (~630,000), as well as the only sovereign Dutch-speaking nation in the Western Hemisphere. Its physical geography is split between a narrow northern coastal plain around the capital Paramaribo—where nearly 90% of residents live—and a vast, roadless Guiana Shield interior blanketed across more than 93% of the national territory by untouched tropical rainforest, the highest forest-cover ratio of any country on Earth. Famously acquired by the Dutch in the 1667 Treaty of Breda in exchange for relinquishing New Amsterdam (modern-day Manhattan, New York) to the British, Suriname remained a Dutch plantation colony for three centuries until achieving independence in 1975.\n\nCenturies of colonial labor importation produced one of the Americas' most extraordinarily diverse societies, blending Hindustani (East Indian), Creole, Javanese (Indonesian), Maroon (descendants of escaped African slaves who forged autonomous jungle communities), Chinese, and Indigenous communities. In 2025–2026, having stabilized its macroeconomy and restructured sovereign debt under an IMF program, Suriname stands on the cusp of a transformative offshore oil boom mirroring neighboring Guyana after TotalEnergies and APA reached a $10.5 billion Final Investment Decision on the Block 58 GranMorgu project (targeting first oil in 2028). Balancing impending petrodollar wealth with its status as one of the world's few certified carbon-negative nations is Paramaribo's defining opportunity.",
