        "All six major islands feature steep, dissected volcanic mountain spines—rising to 2,335 m at Mount Popomanaseu on Guadalcanal—cloaked in dense equatorial rainforest. This rugged archipelagic terrain isolated hundreds of distinct Melanesian communities (speaking ~70 local languages) and made the 1942–1943 Guadalcanal campaign one of the most grueling jungle battlefields of the Second World War."
