    "area": "2,840 km²",
    "sum": "Situated at the geographic and cultural heart of Polynesia—settled by Lapita navigators some 3,000 years ago—the Independent State of Samoa comprises two large volcanic islands, Upolu (home to the capital Apia and three-quarters of the ~225,000 population) and Savai'i (one of the largest islands in Polynesia), along with eight smaller islets. Partitioned by great-power rivalry in 1899 when Imperial Germany annexed the western islands and the United States took the eastern harbor of Pago Pago (American Samoa), Western Samoa was seized by New Zealand troops at the outbreak of World War I in 1914 and administered under League of Nations and UN mandates. In 1962, Samoa made history as the first Pacific Island nation to regain full sovereign independence (dropping \"Western\" from its official name in 1997).\n\nSamoa's political and social fabric uniquely blends Westminster parliamentary democracy with \"Fa'a Samoa\" (the Samoan Way), in which traditional extended-family chiefs (Matai) govern village life, oversee more than 80% of land held under customary communal tenure, and hold seats in Parliament. In December 2011, Samoa famously \"jumped forward in time\" across the International Date Line—skipping December 30, 2011 altogether—to align its business calendar with its primary trade and diaspora partners, New Zealand and Australia! In 2024–2026, led by its first female Prime Minister Fiame Naomi Mata'afa, Samoa elevated its global diplomatic profile by hosting the October 2024 Commonwealth Heads of Government Meeting (CHOGM)—the first ever held in a Pacific Small Island Developing State—while relying on diaspora remittances (~28% of GDP) and tourism.",
