    "pop": "~107K",
    "area": "747 km²",
    "sum": "Stretching roughly 800 km north-to-south across the South Pacific between Fiji and Niue, the Kingdom of Tonga comprises 171 islands (45 inhabited) grouped into three main clusters—Tongatapu, Ha'apai, and Vava'u—where more than 70% of its ~107,000 citizens live on the flat coral-limestone main island of Tongatapu. Geologically, the archipelago sits perched on the edge of the 10,882-meter Tonga Trench (the second-deepest ocean abyss on Earth after the Mariana Trench), pairing an eastern chain of raised coral islands with a volatile western arc of active submarine volcanoes. Heir to the medieval Tu'i Tonga maritime empire, Tonga stands unique as the only Pacific Island nation that never formally surrendered its indigenous sovereignty to a foreign colonial power—thanks to King George Tupou I uniting the islands in 1845 and enacting a modern codified constitution in 1875 (signing only a 1900–1970 Treaty of Friendship making it a British protected state).\n\nToday, Tonga remains Oceania's sole indigenous constitutional monarchy, having transitioned since 2010 to a system in which the monarch and hereditary nobles share legislative power with democratically elected commoner representatives. On January 15, 2022, Tonga endured one of the most violent geological events in modern history when the submarine Hunga Tonga–Hunga Ha'apai volcano erupted with a blast hundreds of times more powerful than the Hiroshima atomic bomb, unleashing 15-meter tsunami waves, blanketing the kingdom in toxic ash, and severing its sole undersea fiber-optic cable for five weeks. In 2024–2026—having hosted the 53rd Pacific Islands Forum (PIF) Leaders Meeting in Nuku'alofa in August 2024—Tonga sustains its reconstruction through the world's highest remittance inflows relative to GDP (~45%) while navigating competing infrastructure diplomacy between Western partners and Beijing.",
