    "pop": "~115K",
    "area": "702 km²",
    "sum": "Stretching nearly 2,900 km east-to-west across the Western Pacific Ocean just north of the Equator, the Federated States of Micronesia (FSM) comprises 607 islands grouped into four highly autonomous states—Yap, Chuuk, Pohnpei (home to the federal capital Palikir), and Kosrae—each with its own distinct language and cultural traditions. Although the combined land area of all 607 islands is just 702 km² (roughly the size of Singapore), their vast geographic dispersion gives the federation a 2.99-million-km² maritime Exclusive Economic Zone straddling the ocean expanse between Guam and Hawaii. Ruled successively by Spain (until 1899), Imperial Germany (1899–1914), and Imperial Japan (1914–1945—which fortified Chuuk/Truk Lagoon as its primary central Pacific fleet base in World War II), the islands became part of the US-administered UN Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands in 1947 before achieving sovereign independence in 1986.\n\nThe geopolitical and economic bedrock of the FSM is its Compact of Free Association (COFA) with the United States, which the US Congress formally renewed for another 20 years in March 2024 alongside $3.3 billion in economic trust and grant assistance. Under the COFA framework, the United States assumes full responsibility for the defense of Micronesia and exercises \"strategic denial\"—barring any third-country military forces from nearly 3 million km² of air and sea space without Washington's consent—while FSM citizens enjoy the right to live, study, and work visa-free in the United States. In 2025–2026, as the Pentagon invests hundreds of millions of dollars upgrading Yap's airfield and port facilities, Palikir's central challenges are managing decentralized state-level politics, climate resilience, and the steady emigration of working-age citizens to the US.",
