    "pop": "~38K",
    "area": "181 km²",
    "sum": "Situated in the central Pacific Ocean roughly halfway between Hawaii and the Philippines, the Republic of the Marshall Islands comprises 29 low-lying coral atolls and five isolated islands arranged in two parallel chains: the eastern Ratak (\"Sunrise\") chain and the western Ralik (\"Sunset\") chain. For millennia, indigenous Marshallese navigators achieved renown for crafting intricate coconut-frond and cowrie-shell \"stick charts\" (rebbelib and mattang) that mapped ocean wave refraction patterns around unseen atolls, enabling pinpoint open-ocean voyaging without instruments. Captured from Imperial Japan by US forces in 1944, the archipelago bore a heavy Cold War scar between 1946 and 1958 when the United States conducted 67 atmospheric nuclear and thermonuclear weapons tests at Bikini and Enewetak Atolls—including the 15-megaton 1954 Castle Bravo hydrogen bomb test, 1,000 times more powerful than the Hiroshima bomb.\n\nSovereign since 1986 under a Compact of Free Association (COFA) with the United States—renewed in March 2024 for another 20 years with $2.3 billion in US funding—the Marshall Islands occupies an irreplaceable niche in American strategic deterrence. Kwajalein Atoll hosts the US Army's Ronald Reagan Ballistic Missile Defense Test Site (RTS), the premier telemetry and radar facility targeting the central lagoon where unarmed Minuteman III intercontinental ballistic missiles (ICBMs) launched 7,700 km away at Vandenberg Space Force Base in California splash down during interceptor tests! In 2025–2026, this nation of ~42,000 people stands as one of four Pacific states maintaining official diplomatic ties with Taiwan and operates the world's third-largest merchant shipping flag registry, even as rising seas threaten its two-meter-high coral islands.",
