    "pop": "~18K",
    "area": "459 km²",
    "sum": "Positioned at the westernmost edge of Micronesia in the Philippine Sea—just 800 km east of Mindanao in the Philippines and northern Indonesia—the Republic of Palau is an archipelago of more than 340 volcanic and uplifted limestone islands. Its physical geography is anchored by mountainous Babeldaob Island (which makes up nearly 80% of the 459 km² landmass and hosts the tiny purpose-built administrative capital of Ngerulmud), the commercial hub of Koror, and the UNESCO-listed \"Rock Islands\" Southern Lagoon—a breathtaking maze of hundreds of mushroom-shaped emerald limestone islets ringed by a vast barrier reef. Following colonial rule by Spain, Germany, and Imperial Japan—and the ferocious 1944 World War II Battle of Peleliu—Palau enacted the world's first nuclear-free constitution in 1979 before entering a Compact of Free Association (COFA) with the United States upon full independence in 1994.\n\nHome to just ~18,000 residents yet boasting one of the Pacific's highest per-capita incomes (~$17,200), Palau is a global trailblazer in marine conservation, requiring every arriving visitor to sign the \"Palau Pledge\" stamped in their passport and designating 80% of its 500,000 km² Exclusive Economic Zone as a no-take National Marine Sanctuary. In 2024–2026, having renewed its COFA pact with Washington for another 20 years alongside $889 million in US economic assistance, Palau serves as the critical southern anchor of the \"Second Island Chain\" linking Guam to Southeast Asia. Under President Surangel Whipps Jr., Palau is hosting the construction of a major US Air Force Tactical Multi-Mission Over-the-Horizon Radar (TACMOR) system, rehabilitating WWII airfields on Peleliu and Angaur, and standing firm as one of Taiwan's four Pacific diplomatic allies despite Beijing's tourism restrictions and cyber operations.",
