    "pop": "~44K",
    "area": "464 km²",
    "sum": "Stretching over 700 km in a volcanic arc directly north of Guam along the rim of the Mariana Trench, the Commonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands (CNMI) is a US territory comprising 14 islands—where 99% of its ~47,000 residents live on the three southern raised-limestone islands of Saipan (the capital), Tinian, and Rota. Sharing an indigenous Chamorro (and Carolinian) heritage with Guam, the northern islands were historically split from Guam in 1899 when Spain sold them to Imperial Germany, before Imperial Japan seized the archipelago in 1914 and developed extensive sugarcane plantations and fortifications. In the summer of 1944, the pivotal US amphibious capture of Saipan and Tinian pierced Japan's inner defense ring, allowing American engineers to build Tinian's massive North Field—the world's busiest airbase in 1945 and the historic launchpad from which the B-29 bombers Enola Gay and Bockscar took off in August 1945 to drop the atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki.\n\nFollowing three decades as part of the US-administered UN Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands, voters in the Northern Marianas approved a 1975 plebiscite by 78.8% to reject separate independence and enter permanent political union with the United States as a US Commonwealth (whose native-born residents hold full US citizenship). In 2024–2026, as Saipan struggles with a steep fiscal and tourism downturn following the collapse of its Chinese casino boom and slow post-pandemic Asian flight recovery, the archipelago's strategic defense importance to the Pentagon has surged. US Air Force engineers are actively clearing jungle to restore all four 2,600-meter World War II runways at Tinian's North Field alongside an expanded Tinian International Airport divert airfield, turning the Northern Marianas into a critical \"Agile Combat Employment\" dispersal hub backing up neighboring Guam.",
