    "area": "199 km²",
    "sum": "Situated in the central South Pacific just 70 km east of the independent state of Samoa—yet separated by the International Date Line so that it sits 24 hours behind its neighbor—American Samoa is the only inhabited territory of the United States in the Southern Hemisphere, comprising five rugged volcanic islands (Tutuila, Aunu'u, and the Manu'a group) and two coral atolls totaling 199 km². The geographic crown jewel of the territory is Pago Pago Harbor on the main island of Tutuila: a dramatic, dogleg-shaped drowned volcanic caldera that forms the deepest and most cyclone-sheltered natural harbor anywhere in the South Pacific. Eager to secure Pago Pago as a strategic naval coaling station along the trans-Pacific sea lane between San Francisco and Australia, the United States signed the 1899 Tripartite Convention partitioning the Samoan archipelago with Imperial Germany and subsequently accepted the 1900 and 1904 Deeds of Cession from indigenous Samoan chiefs (Matai).\n\nConstitutionally, American Samoa (~45,000 residents) holds a unique status as an unincorporated, unorganized US territory whose native-born residents are \"US Nationals\" rather than automatic US citizens—holding US passports and full rights to live and work anywhere in the United States, while requiring naturalization to vote in mainland elections. Local traditional leaders and the territorial government (the Fono) have actively defended this status in US federal courts to protect the traditional \"Fa'a Samoa\" communal land system, which restricts ownership of >90% of island land strictly to persons of at least 50% native Samoan blood (a rule that might otherwise be struck down under the US Constitution's Equal Protection Clause). At the same time, American Samoa consistently records the highest per-capita US military enlistment rate of any state or territory under the American flag, while anchoring its domestic economy on the massive StarKist tuna cannery on Pago Pago Bay.",
