    "sum": "The Cayman Islands comprise three low-lying coral limestone islands—Grand Cayman, Cayman Brac, and Little Cayman—situated in the western Caribbean Sea roughly 240 km south of Cuba and 270 km northwest of Jamaica. First sighted by Christopher Columbus in 1503 and named \"Las Tortugas\" for their abundant sea turtles, the islands were ceded to Britain under the 1670 Treaty of Madrid and governed alongside Jamaica until choosing to remain a separate British dependency in 1962. Rooted in a proud tradition of tax neutrality dating back to King George III's legendary 1794 royal decree following the \"Wreck of the Ten Sail,\" the territory has never levied income or capital taxes.\n\nThis zero-direct-tax regime, paired with political stability and English Common Law courts, has transformed a 264-km² island chain into the world's premier hedge-fund domicile and fifth-largest international banking hub. In 2025–2026, following its removal from the FATF financial-monitoring grey list, Cayman oversees more than 70% of global offshore hedge funds representing over $8 trillion in net assets. With a GDP per capita approaching $100,000, the territory also leads the Western Hemisphere in luxury resort tourism and global superyacht registration.",
