    "pop": "~46K",
    "area": "948 km²",
    "sum": "The Turks and Caicos Islands (TCI) form a British Overseas Territory at the southeastern end of the Bahamas geological platform, sitting in the Lucayan Archipelago roughly 150 km north of Hispaniola (Haiti and the Dominican Republic). Divided into two island groups—the smaller Turks Islands and the larger Caicos Bank—by the 2,200-meter-deep Turks Island Passage, the territory encompasses some 40 low-lying coral islands and cays, of which only eight are inhabited. Historically sustained for over two centuries by solar sea-salt raking and formerly administered via the Bahamas and Jamaica, TCI became a distinct British territory in 1973 and uses the US dollar as its official currency.\n\nToday, the island of Providenciales and its world-renowned Grace Bay beach have propelled Turks and Caicos into one of the Caribbean's fastest-growing ultra-luxury resort and private-villa markets. At the same time, its geographic position directly north of crisis-stricken Haiti has placed TCI on the front line of Caribbean maritime security during 2024–2026, confronting irregular migrant boats and transnational gang spillovers. Britain, the United States Coast Guard, and the Bahamas now conduct coordinated naval and aerial patrols to safeguard the islands and the vital Caicos shipping passages.",
