    "pop": "~4.4K (~5.4K across the three-island territory)",
    "area": "394 km²",
    "sum": "The British Overseas Territory of Saint Helena, Ascension and Tristan da Cunha spans three isolated volcanic island groups scattered thousands of kilometers apart along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, lying nearly 2,000 km west of Africa and 3,000 km east of South America. Discovered by Portuguese navigators in 1502 and fortified by the English East India Company in 1659 as a vital freshwater and provisioning oasis for sailing ships rounding the Cape of Good Hope prior to the Suez Canal, Saint Helena is one of Britain's oldest overseas possessions. Its sheer basalt cliffs and extreme oceanic isolation famously led Britain in 1815 to choose Saint Helena as the inescapable island prison for exiled French Emperor Napoleon Bonaparte until his death at Longwood House in 1821.\n\nIn the modern era, this tri-island territory plays a quiet but pivotal strategic role: Ascension Island to the north hosts the joint UK–US Wideawake Airfield and space-tracking stations, while Tristan da Cunha to the south is the most remote inhabited archipelago on Earth. On Saint Helena itself, the opening of its first commercial airport in 2017—ending five centuries of reliance on mail ships—and the activation of Google's Equiano subsea fiber-optic cable in 2023–2024 have finally connected the island to the modern global economy. Through 2025–2026, alongside Napoleonic heritage tourism and rare specialty coffee exports, the three islands safeguard over 1.6 million km² of South Atlantic marine sanctuaries.",
