        "Saint Helena is the eroded summit of a volcanic seamount rising from 4,000-meter ocean depths, ringed almost entirely by sheer basalt sea cliffs hundreds of meters high that open to the sea only through the narrow ravine of Jamestown. This natural mid-ocean fortress was so inaccessible that the island did not gain its first airport—carved dramatically into a cliffside plateau—until 2017, ending centuries of five-day voyages aboard the Royal Mail Ship St Helena from Cape Town."
