    "area": "60 km²",
    "sum": "The British Indian Ocean Territory (BIOT)—geographically synonymous with the Chagos Archipelago—comprises seven coral atolls and more than 55 low-lying islands situated at the exact geographic center of the Indian Ocean, roughly 1,600 km south of India and south of the Maldives. Charted by Portuguese explorers in the early 16th century and administered alongside Mauritius first by France and then by Britain after 1814, the archipelago is anchored by Diego Garcia, a footprint-shaped coral atoll enclosing a sheltered 21-km-long deep-water lagoon. In 1965, shortly before granting Mauritius independence, Britain detached the Chagos islands to form BIOT and between 1968 and 1973 forcibly relocated all ~1,500–2,000 indigenous Chagossians so the United States could construct a strategic military base on Diego Garcia.\n\nFor over half a century, Naval Support Facility Diego Garcia has served as the Pentagon's premier \"unsinkable aircraft carrier\" in the Indo-Pacific, launching B-52, B-1B, and B-2 stealth bombers and berthing nuclear-powered submarines within striking range of the Middle East, South Asia, and the South China Sea. Following decades of International Court of Justice (ICJ) rulings and UN diplomatic pressure, the UK and Mauritius reached a landmark political agreement in October 2024 and signed a historic treaty in 2025 transferring sovereignty over the Chagos Archipelago to Mauritius. Crucially, the treaty grants Britain and the United States an initial 99-year lease (extendable by 40 years) exercising full operational control over Diego Garcia, locking in Western military preeminence in the Indian Ocean for the next century.",
