    "pop": "~5.8K",
    "area": "242 km²",
    "sum": "Saint-Pierre and Miquelon is a self-governing Territorial Collectivity of France comprising eight rocky islands in the northwestern Atlantic Ocean, perched at the mouth of the Gulf of St. Lawrence just 20 km off the Burin Peninsula of Newfoundland, Canada. Sighted by Portuguese explorer João Álvares Fagundes in 1520 and settled in the early 17th century by French fishermen from Normandy, Brittany, and the Basque Country, the islands changed hands repeatedly between Britain and France before being permanently restored to France under the 1814 Treaty of Paris. Following France's loss of Canada in 1763 and the 1803 Louisiana Purchase, this 242-km² archipelago stands as the sole surviving territorial remnant of the vast colonial empire of \"New France\" in North America.\n\nDuring the era of US Prohibition (1920–1933), French legal exemptions turned Saint-Pierre into the booming liquor-smuggling capital of the North Atlantic, where figures like Al Capone warehoused millions of cases of Canadian whisky and French champagne for fast rum-runner boats bound for the US coast! In 1992, the islands suffered a twin blow when the historic Grand Banks cod fishery collapsed and an international arbitration tribunal awarded France only a narrow, bizarrely shaped \"keyhole and stovepipe\" Exclusive Economic Zone completely surrounded by Canadian waters. Today (2025–2026), this outpost of ~5,800 citizens remains a slice of authentic France in North America—using the euro, European license plates, and Basque pelota courts while pivoting toward snow-crab processing and Francophone heritage tourism.",
