    "area": "18,600 km²",
    "sum": "New Caledonia (*Nouvelle-Calédonie*) is a special-status collectivity of France in the Melanesian southwest Pacific, lying in the Coral Sea roughly 1,200 km east of Australia and 1,500 km northwest of New Zealand. A geological fragment of the ancient supercontinent Gondwana that broke away 80 million years ago, the 18,575-km² territory comprises the 400-km-long main island of Grande Terre, the Loyalty Islands, and the Isle of Pines, all encircled by a 1,500-km UNESCO World Heritage barrier reef enclosing the world's largest lagoon. Named by Captain James Cook in 1774 because its pine-studded ridges reminded him of Scotland (Caledonia) and annexed by France in 1853, the archipelago sits atop 10–20% of the planet's known nickel reserves—a critical mineral for stainless steel and electric-vehicle batteries.\n\nDuring 2024–2026, New Caledonia has stood at the epicenter of Indo-Pacific geopolitics after longstanding tensions between the indigenous Melanesian Kanak community (~41% of the population, largely pro-independence) and European-descended loyalists erupted into severe civil unrest in Nouméa in May 2024 over a proposed French voting-roll reform. The upheaval coincided with an acute structural crisis across the island's energy-intensive nickel smelters caused by surging low-cost Indonesian nickel supply. Through 2025–2026, Paris and local leaders have pursued landmark institutional negotiations establishing deeper shared sovereignty within the French Republic while rebuilding the economy and safeguarding France's primary military bastion in the southwest Pacific.",
