    "area": "7,700 km² (excluding the Adélie Land Antarctic claim)",
    "sum": "The French Southern and Antarctic Lands (TAAF—*Terres australes et antarctiques françaises*) constitute a sprawling Overseas Territory of France divided into five administrative districts stretching from the tropical Mozambique Channel down to the storm-lashed sub-Antarctic Indian Ocean and the Antarctic continent. Its four internationally recognized island districts comprise the Kerguelen Archipelago, the Crozet Islands, Saint-Paul and Amsterdam Islands in the southern Indian Ocean, and the tropical \"Scattered Islands\" (*Îles Éparses*) around Madagascar, while the fifth district—Adélie Land (432,000 km²)—represents France's territorial claim in Antarctica under the 1959 Antarctic Treaty. Discovered largely by 18th-century French navigators Yves-Joseph de Kerguelen and Marc-Joseph Marion du Fresne in 1772, the 7,700 km² of island landmass has zero permanent civilian population, hosting instead a rotating contingent of 150 to 300 scientists, meteorologists, and French military personnel.\n\nDespite being uninhabited, TAAF is the hidden geographic engine behind France's status as one of the world's two largest maritime powers, generating over 2.2 million km² of Exclusive Economic Zone across the Indian Ocean! In the tropics, the Scattered Islands enable French forces to monitor the vital Mozambique Channel supertanker highway (amid ongoing sovereignty claims by Madagascar and Mauritius), while sub-Antarctic Kerguelen hosts strategic European space-tracking telemetry antennas (CNES) for Ariane rockets. Designated a UNESCO World Heritage site and expanded through 2024–2026 into a 1.6-million-km² marine reserve, TAAF safeguards the largest king penguin and albatross colonies on Earth.",
