    "sum": "Norfolk Island is an external territory of Australia situated in the South Pacific Ocean at the apex of the triangle between Australia, New Zealand, and New Caledonia, roughly 1,670 km northeast of Sydney. Covering just 35 km², this lush volcanic plateau is famed worldwide for the towering, symmetrical Norfolk Island pine (*Araucaria heterophylla*), which forms the centerpiece of the territory's flag. First sighted by Captain James Cook in 1774, the island served twice as a notoriously harsh British penal settlement (1788–1814 and 1825–1855) before Queen Victoria gifted the abandoned island in 1856 as a new homeland for all 194 descendants of the *HMS Bounty* mutineers relocated from overcrowded Pitcairn Island!\n\nTransferred from Britain to Australian administration in 1914, Norfolk Islanders preserved their distinctive Anglo-Tahitian heritage and *Norfuk* language under wide local self-government enacted in 1979. Following severe fiscal deficits after the 2008 global financial crisis, however, the Australian Parliament abolished the island's Legislative Assembly in 2015–2016 and integrated Norfolk Island directly into Australia's federal tax, social-welfare, and Medicare systems—a move that sparked enduring local debate over self-determination. In 2024–2026, Canberra is introducing a reformed local governance assembly to restore elected community representation while stewarding the island's UNESCO convict heritage and its strategic 428,000-km² Exclusive Economic Zone.",
