    "area": "372 km²",
    "sum": "Heard Island and McDonald Islands (HIMI) form an uninhabited external territory of Australia located in the storm-swept sub-Antarctic Southern Ocean roughly halfway between Madagascar and Antarctica, more than 4,100 km southwest of Perth, Western Australia. Perched atop the underwater Kerguelen Plateau some 450 km southeast of France's Kerguelen Islands, the 372-km² territory was sighted within weeks of each other in late 1853 and early 1854 by American sea captain John Heard and British captain William McDonald. Transferred from the United Kingdom to Australia in 1947 so Canberra could establish a brief post-war scientific station, the islands were inscribed in 1997 as a UNESCO World Heritage site.\n\nGeologically, Heard Island and McDonald Islands are extraordinary because they contain the only active volcanoes in the entire sub-Antarctic region—and the only active volcanoes on Australian territory! Dominated by the glacier-draped Big Ben volcanic massif, Heard Island's summit of Mawson Peak soars to 2,745 meters above the icy surf—making it 517 meters taller than Mount Kosciuszko (2,228 m), the highest mountain on the Australian mainland! In 2024–2026, the Australian Government expanded the Heard Island and McDonald Islands Marine Reserve nearly fourfold to cover ~390,000 km² (roughly 90% of the territory's Exclusive Economic Zone), safeguarding the only sub-Antarctic island group on Earth completely free of human-introduced alien plants or predators.",
