    "area": "5 km² (~199 km² within the reef flats)",
    "sum": "The Territory of Ashmore and Cartier Islands is an uninhabited external territory of Australia comprising four low-lying coral sand islets spread across two separate reef formations—Ashmore Reef and Cartier Island, 70 km apart—at the outer edge of the Sahul Shelf in the Timor Sea. Geographically, this Australian outpost sits right on the maritime doorstep of Indonesia: Ashmore Reef (known for centuries to Indonesian mariners as *Pulau Pasir*) lies just 140 km south of Indonesia's Rote Island, compared with 320 km off the Kimberley coast of Western Australia. Charted by British captains Nash (aboard the *Cartier* in 1800) and Samuel Ashmore (in 1811) and annexed by Britain in the late 19th century for guano phosphate mining, the two reefs were formally transferred to Australian sovereignty by a 1931 British Order in Council.\n\nIn Canberra's contemporary maritime security architecture (2025–2026), Ashmore and Cartier Islands serve a vital dual strategic role: anchoring Australia's continental-shelf boundaries across the oil- and gas-rich Timor Sea (adjacent to the Bonaparte Basin and the Jabiru and Challis fields) while acting as Australia's northernmost maritime border picket. Within Ashmore Lagoon, Australian Border Force (ABF) and Royal Australian Navy patrol vessels maintain a near-continuous year-round presence under Operation Sovereign Borders to intercept unauthorized maritime arrivals and illegal fishing boats. At the same time, under a historic 1974 bilateral Memorandum of Understanding (the \"MOU Box\"), traditional Indonesian fishermen using non-motorized wooden sailing vessels (*perahu*) retain customary rights to shelter at West Island lagoon and visit ancestral graves.",
